# Tally configuration — every value here is optional.
#
# A Tally container with no environment set at all runs on SQLite and works.
# Copy this file only if you want to change something.
#
#   cp .env.example .env

# Port the app listens on inside the container.
PORT=3000

# Where the SQLite database and all uploaded files live. Uploads are stored here
# on Postgres too, so this volume matters either way.
DATA_DIR=/app/data


# ─── Postgres (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Leave every variable below unset to use the built-in SQLite database.
#
# To use Postgres, set EITHER the connection string OR the four discrete
# variables — never both, and never only some of the four. Tally refuses to
# start on a partial configuration rather than quietly falling back to SQLite,
# because falling back would write your data somewhere you did not intend.
#
# If SQLite data already exists when you switch, Tally will ask in the browser
# whether to import it. Nothing is copied until you say so.
#
# See: https://github.com/LukanRocks/tally#database-configuration

# DATABASE_URL=postgres://tally:secret@db.lan:5432/tally

# ...or, equivalently:
# DB_HOST=db.lan
# DB_NAME=tally
# DB_USER=tally
# DB_PASSWORD=secret
# DB_PORT=5432

# TLS to the database: disable (default), require, or verify-full.
#
# `require` encrypts but does not check which server answered. `verify-full`
# checks the certificate against the system trust store, so it rejects the
# self-signed certificates most homelab setups use.
#
# See: https://github.com/LukanRocks/tally#database-ssl
# DB_SSL=disable
